QB.add(84,[{"n":1,"q":"A 62-year-old male with a history of smoking presents with chronic cough, weight loss, and recent hemoptysis. A chest X-ray is done and is provided below. Which of the following is the most likely primary source of this lesion?","o":[{"t":"Renal cell carcinoma"},{"t":"Breast carcinoma"},{"t":"Gastric adenocarcinoma"},{"t":"Thyroid carcinoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Renal cell carcinoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Renal cell carcinoma is a common cause of cannonball metastases due to its vascular nature and tendency for hematogenous spread. It presents as large, well-defined pulmonary nodules."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with RCC commonly have associated symptoms such as hematuria, flank pain, or palpable mass, but lung metastases can often be the initial presentation in advanced cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The cannonball appearance is characteristic of metastatic RCC on chest imaging."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cannonball Metastases:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cannonball metastases are radiographic findings characterized by multiple large, rounded, well-circumscribed opacities within the lungs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The term is derived from the appearance of these lesions, which resemble large, rounded cannonballs in chest X-rays and CT scans."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Etiology and Common Primary Tumors : Cannonball metastases are associated with cancers prone to hematogenous spread. The most common primary malignancies known to cause cannonball metastases in the lungs include:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mnemonic: CRESP"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C: Choriocarcinoma/colorectal carcinoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"R: Renal cell carcinoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"E: Endometrial carcinoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S: Synovial sarcoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P: Prostate carcinoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Clinical Presentation : Symptoms can be varied and include chronic cough, hemoptysis, dyspnea, weight loss, and fatigue. In some cases, cannonball metastases are incidentally discovered on imaging during evaluation for other symptoms or known malignancies."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Pathophysiology : The development of cannonball metastases results from the hematogenous spread of tumor cells, allowing them to bypass lymphatic pathways and form nodular deposits directly in the lung parenchyma. This pattern reflects the aggressive nature of certain tumors and their tendency for vascular invasion."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4. Imaging :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"X-ray : Multiple large, round, sharply defined opacities scattered throughout the lung fields."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT Scan : Better delineates the size, shape, and distribution of these nodules, allowing for assessment of the primary and any additional thoracic involvement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Breast Carcinoma (Option B): Breast carcinoma is one of the most common malignancies to metastasize to the lungs, but its pulmonary metastases often present as ill-defined or pleural-based lesions rather than as large, well-circumscribed nodules."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastric Adenocarcinoma (Option C): Gastric cancer can spread to the lungs, but this usually occurs as lymphangitic spread or multiple small nodules rather than large, rounded lesions. Cannonball metastases are atypical for gastric cancer as this more frequently spreads through peritoneal seeding or involves lymph nodes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thyroid Carcinoma (Option D): Thyroid carcinoma, particularly follicular type, can metastasize to the lungs and other distant organs; however, it generally presents with smaller, less distinct pulmonary nodules rather than cannonball lesions. Cannonball metastases are not typical of thyroid malignancies."}],"qi":[10152]},{"n":2,"q":"A 65-year-old male with a history of chronic liver disease presents with progressive shortness of breath, fatigue, and mild chest discomfort over the past few weeks. On examination, there is decreased bilateral breath sound intensity with dullness on percussion at the lung bases. A chest X-ray shows the following features. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Bilateral Hydrothorax"},{"t":"Empyema Thoracis"},{"t":"Hydropneumothorax"},{"t":"Congestive Heart Failure with Pleural Effusion"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Bilateral Hydrothorax"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In this case, the clinical picture and imaging findings suggest a diagnosis of bilateral hydrothorax, particularly given the patient’s background of chronic liver disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic hydrothorax can develop in patients with advanced liver disease due to transudative fluid accumulation, often through diaphragmatic defects, into the pleural spaces."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The absence of air-fluid levels, along with bilaterally homogeneous pleural fluid without signs of infection, supports the diagnosis of hydrothorax over other options."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bilateral Hydrothorax:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic hydrothorax is a pleural effusion often associated with advanced liver disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It presents as transudative pleural effusions, most commonly on the right but can be bilateral. Pathophysiologically, ascitic fluid moves across diaphragmatic defects into the pleural space due to pressure gradients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilateral hydrothorax, characterized by transudative fluid accumulation in the pleural space on both sides, can be caused by several other conditions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common causes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In each case, imaging and clinical features can help distinguish the underlying cause, and pleural fluid analysis (e.g., protein, LDH levels) can determine if the effusion is transudative (as in hydrothorax) or exudative."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Empyema Thoracis (Option B):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Empyema thoracis involves infection in the pleural space, leading to the accumulation of pus, which may be accompanied by loculations. Patients often present with fever, pleuritic chest pain, and general malaise."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"On imaging, empyema may present as pleural effusions with loculated areas, often with a thickened pleura and fluid that may appear dense due to pus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Notably, empyema may show a “ split pleura sign ” on imaging, which is absent in hydrothorax."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This patient lacks systemic signs of infection and shows no loculation, making empyema less likely."},{"k":"img","v":10222},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fig: Left-sided biconvex pleural collection along the lateral chest wall. The obtuse angle with the chest wall indicates this arising from the pleural space."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hydropneumothorax (Option C):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hydropneumothorax is characterized by the presence of both air and fluid within the pleural space, usually due to trauma, rupture of a lung abscess, or after thoracic interventions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hydropneumothorax shows an air-fluid level on upright imaging, with a clear demarcation between air and fluid in the pleural cavity ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, there are no air-fluid levels seen on imaging, and the clinical background of chronic liver disease with effusions bilaterally is inconsistent with hydropneumothorax."},{"k":"img","v":10227},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Congestive Heart Failure with Pleural Effusion (Option D):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CHF can lead to pleural effusion due to increased hydrostatic pressure, presenting as transudative effusion, more common on the right side or bilateral."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms include dyspnea, orthopnea, and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pleural effusions in CHF are often accompanied by features of pulmonary edema, including perihilar congestion, interstitial markings, and cardiomegaly ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Although CHF may cause bilateral pleural effusions, the patient’s primary history of chronic liver disease and absence of signs of cardiac dysfunction make hepatic hydrothorax a more probable diagnosis."}],"qi":[10158]},{"n":3,"q":"A 65-year-old man visits OPD with the following presentation as shown in the image. His chest CT shows bronchiectasis. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT regarding this patient's digital finding?","o":[{"t":"The condition involves proliferation of connective tissue at the nail base"},{"t":"It can be associated with cystic fibrosis and lung cancer"},{"t":"The condition always indicates irreversible tissue changes"},{"t":"The pathogenesis involves platelet-derived growth factors"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) The condition always indicates irreversible tissue changes"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The image shows typical findings of clubbing of fingers, seen in various cardiopulmonary conditions. Clubbing is not always irreversible and can be reversible in certain conditions."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clubbing of Fingers"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition • Enlargement of distal segments of fingers and toes"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Proliferation of connective tissue (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Particularly on dorsal surface"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Changes at base of clubbed nail"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology • Connective tissue proliferation"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Exact mechanism unknown"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Platelet-derived growth factors implicated (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Signs Bulbous Enlargement of the Distal Phalanges"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Loss of the Normal Angle Between the Nail and the Nailbed (Lovibond’s Angle) Increased Sponginess of the Soft Tissue at the Base of the Nail Nail Changes: Nail flattening / nail curvature Hippocratic fingers : The tips of the fingers become enlarged, and the nails curve downward"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical tests Schamroth’s Sign:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Normal : A small diamond-shaped window should be visible between the nails when the fingers are positioned in this way. Positive Schamroth’s Sign : The window disappears, and there is no gap between the nails, indicating the loss of the normal angle at the nail bed."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Associated • Cyanotic congenital heart disease Conditions • Infective endocarditis"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Bronchiectasis"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Cystic Fibrosis (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Lung cancer"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Tuberculosis"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Inflammatory bowel disease"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Key Features • Can be hereditary or acquired"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"May be reversible in certain circumstances"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Associated with hypertrophic osteoarthropathy"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Can cause pain and arthritis-like changes"}],"qi":[10163]},{"n":4,"q":"A 25-year-old male presents to the emergency department with sudden onset of right-sided chest pain and mild shortness of breath. He is tall, thin, and a smoker. Physical examination reveals decreased breath sounds on the right side with hyperresonance on percussion. Chest X-ray is given below. The patient is hemodynamically stable with oxygen saturation of 96% on room air. What is the most appropriate initial management?","o":[{"t":"Discharge with follow-up in 2-4 weeks"},{"t":"Needle aspiration"},{"t":"Immediate tube thoracostomy"},{"t":"High-flow oxygen and observation for 24 hours"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Needle aspiration"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: This case presents a primary spontaneous pneumothorax (PSP) in a typical patient (young, tall, thin male who smokes). The key factors determining management are:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest radiographic image demonstrates classic appearances of right-sided pneumothorax with a readily apparent visceral pleural line seen without distal lung markings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of symptoms (chest pain and mild SOB)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemodynamic stability"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type (primary spontaneous)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"According to management guidelines, in PSP:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If size > 2 cm and patient is symptomatic, needle aspiration is the initial step"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If after aspiration the residual depth is < 2 cm and patient improves, they can be discharged"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If aspiration fails, tube thoracostomy is indicated"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Primary and Secondary Pneumothorax:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type Clinical Features Investigations Management Primary Spontaneous • Young, tall, thin • Chest X-ray • Size < 2cm, asymptomati Pneumothorax (PSP) males • CT scan (if c: Observation"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Sharp chest pain needed) • Size > 2cm or"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Mild dyspnea • E-FAST ultrasoun symptomatic: Needle"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Decreased d aspiration breath sounds • Failed aspiration: Tube"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Hyperresonant thoracostomy percussion • Recurrent: Consider"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"VATS/pleurodesis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secondary Spontaneous • Underlying lung • Chest X-ray • Size < 1cm: On + Pneumothorax (SSP) disease • CT scan (mandato observation"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Severe dyspnea ry) • Size 1-2cm: Needle"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Significant chest • ABG analysis aspiration pain • Underlying • Size > 2cm: Tube"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Respiratory disease workup thoracostomy distress • All cases: Treat"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Higher risk of underlying condition tension"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Discharge with follow-up in 2-4 weeks (Option A): Discharge with follow-up is only appropriate for asymptomatic patients with small (<2 cm) pneumothorax"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immediate tube thoracostomy (Option C) is too aggressive as first-line treatment for a stable PSP; it's reserved for failed needle aspiration or unstable patients"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-flow oxygen and observation for 24 hours (Option D): High-flow oxygen and observation is more appropriate for small (<1 cm) secondary spontaneous pneumothorax"}],"qi":[10169]},{"n":5,"q":"A 28-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after a motor vehicle accident with chest trauma. He is in severe respiratory distress with BP 80/50 mmHg, HR 142/min, RR 32/min. Physical examination reveals absent breath sounds. The X-ray is attached below. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","o":[{"t":"CT Head for identifying possible cerebral injury"},{"t":"Start high-flow oxygen"},{"t":"Immediate needle decompression"},{"t":"Intercostal tube"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Immediate needle decompression"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The case presents a classic presentation of tension pneumothorax, characterized by:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe respiratory distress"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension (BP 80/50)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tachycardia (HR 142)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absent breath sounds and hyperresonance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tracheal deviation to contralateral side"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"History of trauma"},{"k":"img","v":10050},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Also, X-ray shows tension pneumothorax with absent lung markings on the lateral side of the right pleural cavity and a collapsed right lung. The trachea and heart are displaced toward the left indicating a mediastinal shift."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of mediastinal shift with hypotension is a medical emergency requiring immediate intervention. The positive pleural pressure and mediastinal shift in tension pneumothorax can lead to cardiovascular collapse if not treated promptly. Tension Pneumothorax"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features • Severe respiratory distress"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Hypotension"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Tachycardia (>134 bpm)"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Tracheal deviation"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"JVD"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Cyanosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations • Clinical diagnosis"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Don't wait for imaging"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Post-procedure CXR"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management • Immediate: Needle decompression"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Definitive: Tube thoracostomy"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Follow-up: Pleurodesis consideration"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CT Head for identifying possible cerebral injury (Option A): Although CT head is to be done according to the protocol, the clinically obvious tension pneumothorax with hemodynamic compromise is a medical emergency and needs to be treated promptly."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-flow oxygen (Option B): Insufficient treatment for tension pneumothorax with hemodynamic compromise"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tube thoracostomy (Option D): While this is the definitive treatment, immediate needle decompression should be performed first in an unstable patient as it's quicker and can provide immediate relief of the tension component"}],"qi":[10050]},{"n":6,"q":"A 68-year-old female receiving a blood transfusion for severe anemia develops acute dyspnea, tachypnea, and hypertension 30 minutes into the transfusion. Her blood pressure is 168/95 mmHg, and chest auscultation reveals bilateral crackles. Which of the following represents the most appropriate immediate management?","o":[{"t":"Continue transfusion but at a slower rate"},{"t":"Stop transfusion and administer diuretics"},{"t":"Administer acetaminophen and continue transfusion"},{"t":"Stop transfusion and start steroids"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Stop transfusion and administer diuretics"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: This case presents classic symptoms of Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TACO is a common and underrecognized transfusion adverse reaction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features include dyspnea, hypertension, bilateral crackles"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Management explicitly requires stopping the transfusion and administration of O2 and diuretics"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Risk factors include pre existing fluid overload and cardiac dysfunction"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition Common and underrecognized transfusion adverse"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"reaction"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incidence 1-10% per RBC transfusion"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Risk Factors • Preexisting fluid overload"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Cardiac dysfunction"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Large volume of RBCs"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Excessive rate of transfusion"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features • Dyspnea"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Hypertension"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Bilateral crackles"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Elevated brain natriuretic peptide"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prevention in High-Risk Patients • Slow transfusion rate (RBCC over 3-4 h)"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Close monitoring"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management • Stop transfusion"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Administer O2"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Give diuretics"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"TRALI:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) is a serious complication that can occur during or within 6 hours after receiving a blood transfusion (or up to 72 hours in delayed cases)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It's characterized by acute, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema with hypoxia, making it the leading cause of transfusion-related deaths reported to the FDA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The condition typically presents with symptoms like fever, hypotension, tachycardia, and difficulty breathing, along with bilateral infiltrates visible on chest X-rays."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TRALI occurs when antibodies in donor blood react with the recipient's blood cells, or when stored blood products accumulate inflammatory mediators, leading to damage in the pulmonary blood vessels and fluid leakage into the lungs."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Continue transfusion but at a slower rate (Option A): Continuing transfusion even at a slower rate would worsen fluid overload"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Administer acetaminophen and continue transfusion (Option C): Acetaminophen would not address the underlying fluid overload"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stop transfusion and start steroids (Option D): Steroids are not indicated in the management of TACO according to the provided information"}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 65-year-old male presents to the emergency department with acute dyspnea and bilateral crackles. His blood pressure is 160/90 mmHg, heart rate 110/min, and respiratory rate 28/min. Physical examination reveals jugular venous distention, bilateral pedal edema, and an S3 gallop. Chest X-ray is given below. His pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) is 25 mmHg. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?","o":[{"t":"High-flow oxygen and observation"},{"t":"Immediate intubation"},{"t":"IV furosemide and nitrates"},{"t":"Antibiotics and steroids"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) IV furosemide and nitrates"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The case presents classic cardiogenic pulmonary edema with characteristic features including elevated PCWP (>18 mmHg), JVD, pedal edema, and S3 gallop. Also the chest X-ray shows cardiomegaly and mild pulmonary edema with widening of the vascular pedicle (red arrow) and interstitial prominence."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cardiogenic and Non-Cardiogenic Pulmonary Edema:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features Cardiogenic Pulmonary Edema Non-cardiogenic Pulmonary Edema"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology • Increased hydrostatic pressure • Increased capillary permeability"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Left heart dysfunction • Direct lung injury"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"PCWP >18 mmHg • Normal PCWP (<18 mmHg)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common Causes • MI/CAD • ARDS"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"CHF • Sepsis"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Valvular heart disease • Trauma"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Cardiomyopathy • Toxins/drugs"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"High altitude"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features • JVD present • No JVD"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Peripheral edema • No peripheral edema"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"S3 gallop • Normal heart sounds"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Cardiomegaly on CXR • Normal heart size on CXR"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Initial Management • Diuretics (furosemide) • Treat underlying cause"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Nitrates • Supportive care"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Proper positioning • Mechanical ventilation if needed"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Oxygen support • No role for diuretics"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-flow oxygen and observation (Option A): High-flow oxygen alone is insufficient for cardiogenic pulmonary edema requiring preload reduction"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immediate intubation (Option B) is not necessary unless non-invasive measures fail"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Antibiotics and steroids (Option D): Antibiotics and steroids are more appropriate for certain types of non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema, but not in cardiogenic pulmonary edema."}],"qi":[10176]},{"n":8,"q":"A 65-year-old male with a 40-pack-year smoking history presents with progressive dyspnea, productive cough, and weight loss. Physical examination reveals a barrel chest, decreased breath sounds, and prolonged expiration. Chest X-ray is given below. His FEV1/FVC ratio is 0.65 and doesn't improve with bronchodilators. Which of the following is the most likely pathological finding in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Inflammation of bronchial walls with mucus hypersecretion"},{"t":"Permanent enlargement of airspaces distal to terminal bronchioles"},{"t":"Bronchial wall thickening with reversible airway obstruction"},{"t":"Interstitial fibrosis with restrictive pattern"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Permanent enlargement of airspaces distal to terminal bronchioles"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario of a smoker with dyspnea, cough, weight loss along with barrel chest and reduced FEV1/FVC ratio with an x-ray image showing hyperinflation & increased lung volume suggests classic emphysema where Permanent enlargement of airspaces distal to terminal bronchioles are seen."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis of emphysema in this case is supported by multiple key findings:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Risk Factors:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"40-pack-year smoking history (significant exposure > 20 pack-years)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age group (65 years) typical for COPD/emphysema presentation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Clinical Features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive dyspnea (characteristic of airspace destruction)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Barrel chest (indicates hyperinflation)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased breath sounds (due to decreased air movement)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prolonged expiration (due to loss of elastic recoil)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight loss (common in emphysema - \"pink puffer\")"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Objective Findings:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lack of improvement with bronchodilators (indicates irreversible obstruction)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray showing severely hyperinflated lungs with emphysematous changes. Also, a flattened diaphragm is seen. Emphysema"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology • Destruction of alveolar walls"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Abnormal permanent enlargement of airspaces"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Elastase/anti-elastase imbalance"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Loss of elastic recoil"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Decreased gas exchange surface area"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Causes • Smoking (80-90% cases)"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (1-2%)"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Environmental pollutants"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Biomass fuel exposure"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Occupational exposure"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features • Progressive dyspnea"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Chronic cough"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Barrel chest"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Pursed-lip breathing"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Weight loss"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"\"Pink puffer\" appearance"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Use of accessory muscles"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Radiological Findings • Hyperinflation"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Flattened diaphragm"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Increased lung volumes"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Tubular heart shadow"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Increased retrosternal airspace"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations • Spirometry (FEV1/FVC < 0.7)"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Chest X-ray"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"CT scan"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin levels"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Arterial blood gases"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"DLCO (decreased)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment • Smoking cessation"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Bronchodilators (SABA, LABA)"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Anticholinergics (SAMA, LAMA)"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Inhaled corticosteroids"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Pulmonary rehabilitation"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Oxygen therapy if indicated"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Surgery in selected cases"},{"k":"b","l":2,"v":"Vaccinations (influenza, pneumococcal)"}],"qi":[10181]},{"n":9,"q":"A 55-year-old man with a history of deep vein thrombosis is brought to the emergency department by his wife after he suddenly developed sharp, stabbing chest pain and shortness of breath while at home. He describes the pain as worse when taking a deep breath. The X-ray taken is displayed below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Pneumonia"},{"t":"Atelectasis"},{"t":"Pulmonary infarction due to pulmonary embolism"},{"t":"Lung abscess"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Pulmonary infarction due to pulmonary embolism"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The wedge-shaped opacity pointing toward the hilum is characteristic of Hampton's Hump , a classic radiologic finding associated with pulmonary infarction due to pulmonary embolism (PE)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The sudden onset of pleuritic chest pain and the patient's history of DVT make PE the most likely diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hampton's Hump is a radiologic sign seen in cases of pulmonary embolism (PE) and is indicative of a pulmonary infarction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seen on chest X-rays as a pleural-based, wedge-shaped opacity ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The apex of the wedge points toward the hilum or occluding vessel."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically not visible within the first 24 hours after the embolus lodges in the pulmonary artery."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Appears later as parenchymal infarction develops, and may take 3–5 weeks or longer to resolve ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A residual band-like opacity due to scarring or pleural thickening may persist."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The infarcted area can sometimes become secondarily infected , leading to cavitation or abscess."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This may also occur due to septic emboli."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hampton’s Hump can also result from edema or hemorrhage rather than infarction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically found in lower lobes and may appear 12 hours to several days after the thromboembolic event, with a faster resolution (7–10 days) compared to infarctions."},{"k":"img","v":10242},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pneumonia (Option A), is incorrect as pneumonia usually presents on X-ray as a patchy or lobar consolidation , often not triangular or pleural-based . Pneumonia can be found in any lobe but is not typically wedge-shaped with an apex pointing toward the hilum."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pneumonia"},{"k":"img","v":10248},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Atelectasis (Option B), is incorrect as atelectasis usually presents on X-ray as linear or wedge-shaped opacities but tends to involve volume loss or shifting of adjacent structures , such as the trachea or mediastinum, toward the affected area."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Pathology\" data-hash=\"8851\" Lung Abscess (Option D), is incorrect as a lung abscess data-license=\"CC-BY-SA-2.0\" data-source=\"https://www. usually presents on x-ray as a localized cavity with an flickr.com/photos/pulmonary_pathology/7468374820\" air-fluid level if communicating with an airway . data-tags=\"\" height=\"267\" src=\"https://image.prepladder. Abscesses are usually round or irregular in shape , not com/notes/8ajgu1swqVUXwUaIEbvV1744309932.png\" wedge-shaped or pleural-based like Hampton’s Hump. width=\"249\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lung abscess"}],"qi":[10187]},{"n":10,"q":"A 65-year-old male patient presents to the pulmonary clinic with a history of progressive shortness of breath and a chronic cough. He is a long-term smoker and has a family history of respiratory diseases. A spirometry test is performed, and the flow-volume loop is plotted. What is the most likely diagnosis based on the patient’s symptoms and the shape of his flow-volume loop?","o":[{"t":"Restrictive lung disease"},{"t":"Obstructive lung disease"},{"t":"Normal lung function"},{"t":"Fixed upper airway obstruction"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Obstructive lung disease"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The scooped-out pattern on expiration is a classic finding in obstructive diseases like COPD, which often presents in smokers with chronic symptoms like the patient."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Flow-volume loops can display distinct patterns, each associated with different types of respiratory conditions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types of Flow-Volume Loops:"},{"k":"img","v":10251},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Flow volume curve loop types and examples:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Loop Type Expiratory Curve Inspiratory Curve Example Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Normal Symmetrical, Symmetrical, Healthy lungs (Option C"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"peaked rounded ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Obstructive Scooped (conca Normal COPD, asthma"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ve)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Restrictive Narrow, peaked Narrow, peaked Pulmonary fibrosis,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pleural effusion (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Fixed Upper Airway Flattened (box-li Flattened (box-lik Tracheal stenosis, large Obstruction ke) e) goiter (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Variable Extrathoracic Normal Flattened Vocal cord dysfunction, Obstruction laryngeal tumor"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Variable Intrathoracic Flattened Normal Tracheomalacia, Obstruction bronchogenic tumor"}],"qi":[10190]},{"n":11,"q":"Match the Condition with the Correct Description on X-ray: 1. Pneumatocele a. 2. Tuberculosis (TB) b. 3. Miliary TB c. 4. Lung Abscess d.\n1. Pneumatocele\n... (content truncated)\n2. Tuberculosis (TB)\nb.\n3. Miliary TB\n... (content truncated)\n4. Lung Abscess\n... (content truncated)","o":[{"t":"1 - b, 2 - c, 3 - a, 4 - d"},{"t":"1 - a, 2 - b, 3 - c, 4 - d"},{"t":"1 - d, 2 - c, 3 - b, 4 - a"},{"t":"1 - c, 2 - b, 3 - d, 4 - a"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: A) 1 - b, 2 - c, 3 - a, 4 - d"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pneumatocele - Image (b)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tuberculosis (TB) - Image (c)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Miliary TB - Image (a)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lung Abscess - Image (d)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pneumatocele (Image b): Appears as a thin-walled, air-filled cyst in the lung, often resulting from trauma, infection (like staphylococcal pneumonia), or positive pressure ventilation. It usually resolves on its own but can occasionally rupture, leading to pneumothorax."},{"k":"img","v":10202},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tuberculosis (Image c): Presents as a localized area of lung consolidation , often in the upper lobe ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Miliary TB (Image a):"},{"k":"img","v":10196},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Appears as multiple small, uniformly distributed nodules throughout both lungs ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a severe, disseminated form of TB where the bacteria spread through the bloodstream, leading to numerous tiny nodules scattered across the lungs and other organs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It gets its name because the nodules resemble millet seeds on imaging . Miliary TB is often seen in immunocompromised individuals."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lung Abscess (Image d): It often appears as a thick-walled cavity with air-fluid levels on imaging."},{"k":"img","v":10212}],"qi":[10196,10202,10207,10212]}]);
